    "content": "The 25th Amendment (Section 4) can be invoked against a President by the Vice President acting together with a majority of the principal officers of the executive departments (the Cabinet). They must submit a written declaration to the President pro tempore of the Senate and the House Speaker stating the President is incapacitated. Key Details on Invoking the 25th Amendment: Purpose: Section 4 allows for the involuntary transfer of power if the President is \"unable to discharge the powers and duties of his office\". The Process: Initiation: The Vice President and a majority of the Cabinet submit a written declaration. Immediate Effect: The Vice President immediately becomes Acting President. Presidential Challenge: If the President declares no incapacity exists, they resume power, unless the Vice President and Cabinet re-contest it within four days. Congressional Action: If contested, Congress must meet within 21 days and decide the issue, requiring a two-thirds vote in both houses to uphold the removal. Scope: The 25th Amendment does not apply to political disagreements, but rather to incapacity (e.g., physical or mental illness). History: Section 4, the involuntary removal clause, has never been invoked in U.S. history. Section 3 (voluntary transfer) has been used multiple times.",
